artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a heterogeneous tumor consisting of drug-sensitive cells and drug-resistant (possibly only partially resistant) cells, whose dynamics over time t >= 0 are governed by a population model that may include: (i) Lotka–Volterra-type competition between sensitive cells S(t) and resistant cells R(t) in which the strength of competitive suppression of R by S grows with total tumor burden N(t) = S(t) + R(t); (ii) drug-induced death of sensitive cells with rate depending on instantaneous drug dose D(t) in [0, D_max], where D_max denotes the maximum tolerated dose; (iii) optional spatial structure, cell mobility, and a quiescent compartment.

An adaptive dosing protocol is a (possibly feedback) rule that prescribes D(t) as a function of past observations of tumor size N(t). Two canonical families are: (a) dose-skipping protocols, in which D(t) ∈ {0, D_max} with switches triggered by thresholds on N(t); and (b) dose-modulation protocols, in which D(t) varies continuously to stabilize N(t) near a chosen target level N* ∈ (0, N_max], where N_max is a clinically tolerable maximum tumor size. A stabilization dose D_stab is a dose that maintains N(t) ≈ N* once reached, and a titration rule specifies how D(t) is updated when N(t) deviates from N*.

Define a performance functional combining: time to progression (loss of tumor control), expected delay of resistance emergence (e.g. time until R(t) exceeds a threshold), cumulative toxicity ∫_0^T D(t) dt or a nonlinear toxicity functional, clinical feasibility (frequency of dose changes, monitoring burden), and robustness to model misspecification (e.g. uncertainty in growth rates, in the degree of resistance, in spatial mixing, and in quiescence).

Problem. Determine an adaptive dosing protocol D(t) = π(N(·)|_{[0,t]}) that simultaneously:
1. optimizes the above multi-criterion performance functional;
2. specifies whether dose-skipping or dose-modulation is preferable, and under what model assumptions;
3. decides whether the optimal target N* should equal N_max (to maximize competitive suppression of resistance) or lie strictly below it;
4. for partially resistant resistant subpopulations, identifies the optimal time τ at which the protocol should switch to constant maximum tolerated dose D_max, characterizing τ in terms of observable quantities (e.g., decreasing treatment efficiency);
5. prescribes a stabilization dose D_stab and a titration rule that adapt to observed changes in N(t);
6. remains effective when the underlying model includes spatial structure, cell motility, quiescent cells, and multiple resistant phenotypes.
